limitations_weaknesses.value:
However, the paper approach raised some concerns that could be important issues. 

The most important is on the design in creating the dataset: the benchmark its called InfoChart but does not capture key infographic properties in the reviewer’s view. Infographic are not only graphs, but a composition of multiple elements, in the same page, such as text, images, multiple graphs, icons etc. All these (perfectly represented in InfoVQA) could be also vew in a different medium type like Slides (SlidesVQA). However, in single charts it is not visible. Thus, AI models like Gemini and GPT-4o seems to already being able to understand the important elements of *some* single charts (chart to table part of the pipeline). The authors retain only those charts that GPT-4o and Gemini can already translate to "perfect" tables, discarding the “hard-parse” diagrams whose semantics depend on unusual layouts, multi-panel references or ambiguous iconography. In other words, the dataset is curated for ease, not difficulty. Text-based questions are produced from rigid templates tied to a single chart, so they probe simple numeric look-ups rather than the cross-component reasoning, co-reference and discourse structure that characterize genuine infographics (e.g., “According to the bubble chart on page 3, which country shown in the flag legend on page 1 has the highest GDP growth?”, or similar). Because of this dual simplification, state-of-the-art proprietary MLLMs already reach ≥90 % of human accuracy on text questions and ~85 % on visual-element questions. The benchmark therefore offers little head-room and risks becoming obsolete as soon as it is released.

A more impactful pipeline would:
- Manually select authentic multi-panel infographics with rich inter-panel links; 
- Crowd-source or expert-write non-templated, co-referential questions for the test split;
- Optionally auto-generate a training split and demonstrate that light finetuning (LoRA, Q-LoRA) yields measurable gains, validating the usefulness of the synthetic portion.
- Keep the “hard-parse” charts (those that today’s MLLMs cannot table-ize) and provide gold tables, so future systems can attack the full end-to-end problem rather than an already-solved sub-task.

More weaknesses are the following:
- Creating questions with a template per diagram/graph type introduce biases. 55k text questions come from a fixed library of templates tied to 11 “data-fact” types, limiting linguistic diversity and probably favoring LLMs pre-exposed to similar wording;
- filtering the all possible scraped diagrams with only the charts intersecting GPT-4o and Gemini parser agree on might discard interesting and complex graphs/plots; thus the hardest diagrams are absent from the final set. This selection is bias toward “solvable” cases;
- the paper correlates accuracy with a post-hoc element count (Fig. 2) but does not publish per-chart scores or define complexity levels in the dataset metadata;
- copyright is not clear: most images are scraped from Pinterest, iStock, Visual Capitalist, etc., yet the data license table lists them simply as “Custom” and the ethics statement asserts that “publicly available data [have] no copyright issue when only used for academic purposes”, which is a legally dubious claim and have to be proven backed by the license of the platform or of the diagrams. 

Minor issues but still to be considered:
- the claims about “metaphor understanding” rest on a statistically small sample (around 3%)
- Table 2 lacks bold/underline cues for best/second-best, slowing comparison

